00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.269 align:start position:0%
hi there steve kaufman here  i want to continue talking about this  importance of being relaxed when we're  learning languages  i think  one of the biggest obstacles to  successful language learning  is stage fright  people think they're performing  when they're speaking a foreign language  we're not performing we're just  communicating  and as long as we're focused on  communicating  we will succeed  and communication  takes place when we're listening when  we're reading when we're speaking when  we're writing  these are all forms of communicating in  in another language and if our focus is  firmly on communicating we will  expose our brains to enough of the  language that we will continue to  improve  i constantly get  letters emails from people to my blog  for example at the linguistic language  or at link  people say you know i've been studying  english or some other language for  so much time and i don't think i'm  making any progress and what should i do  and stuff  and my answer always is just enjoy  the language you will continue to  improve  some people are going to do better some  people are going to have an easier time  pronouncing the language  some people have learned more languages  and therefore might do better  but as long as you're enjoying the  language that's fine mission  accomplished and if you spend the time  with the language  you will improve  and uh  you know there was a discussion uh on  one of our forums at link  someone raised the issue of  of pitch in japanese in fact they were  concerned about uh this person was  concerned about the accents  of some of the content at link whether  it was from osaka or tokyo and  my contention is with all these issues  of accent whether it be in spanish or  portuguese or french or english  it really doesn't matter  uh you'll gradually imitate the form of  the language that you either hear the  most or that you want to  uh imitate but you should in fact expose  yourself to a lot of different accents  it's not going to hurt you  and uh this person felt though that that  was very important to get the pitch  right in japanese and i had never heard  of pitch in japanese i didn't i don't  know what it is  and  this person directed me to some websites  where i saw some tremendously  complicated explanations about pitch  and referring to i think the term was  mora or something which was some other  term other than phoneme it all has to do  with sounds and  and i just think that if  in learning japanese i had to worry  about the pitch  of different words and worry about  whether the person i was speaking to was  using the pitch that i wanted to  imitate i mean it just introduces  another element of complication it's  just like golf the more  uh instruction you have about how to  hold your wrist your knee your shoulder  the less well you can play golf if you  go out there to enjoy the game you're  actually going to do better and you have  to gradually start to feel the swing in  golf and some people in the long run  will do better and some will do less  well  i think in languages the differences are  not as great because in golf you have to  be a very talented  athlete and practice a lot  whereas in language learning we are all  capable of speaking languages but i  think this whole idea of  i mean i learned japanese i never  worried about pitch didn't know it  existed  i don't think i learned one single  grammatical term  in learning japanese i just know that  they say  whatever  whatever it might be  uh and and in speaking japanese  undoubtedly i was always influenced by  the people that i was around so that if  i spent  uh three weeks in the kansai with some  customers down there i might uh come  back speaking with much with more of a  kansai accent and then if i was back in  tokyo then i would lose that it didn't  really matter but i just think if we get  back this idea of stage fright  being such a major inhibiting factor in  language learning the more things we  have to think about  is this the right pitch or in my russian  is this the right case i mean i just let  it come out it'll come out wrong right  and gradually over time it'll come out  right more often and of course i review  the cases in russian but the fewer and  you have to because eventually i want to  speak russian correctly but the fewer  things like that that we introduced  the better the more confident the person  can be that if i have to worry about  pitch it's the same with tones in  chinese  sure when we learn the words we want to  see what tone they are but mostly we  want to get the hang of the rhythm by  listening  and imitating and if i have to think  about the tone of every word in chinese  as i speak then i can't speak  so i guess i just you know it's a bit  like i mentioned in my previous podcast  about this term parasegmentals applied  to learning english the fewer technical  terms we use the fewer technical aspects  of the language that we direct the  learner to try to learn  uh the better  the the fewer inhibitions the learner is  going to have they won't be checking it  for pitch and checking it for tone and  checking it for case and checking it for  this rule and that rule with all the  exceptions  they'll just learn and if they focus on  the meaning focus on communication again  it was the same when i started with  russian  uh the conflict the explanations are  incredibly complicated  i don't say that i don't occasionally  review russian grammar i do  but i don't do it with the intent  of learning it remembering it  i just use the occasional grammar of you  as a reminder and the more i'm exposed  to russian the more natural some of this  stuff starts to become and the more  naturally i'm able to reproduce it when  i speak  so i just because i had these one i had  the one person who emailed me and said  you know i've been learning english for  so long and i still don't feel that i'm  fluent and then this other person  worrying about pitch and this other  person in fact said that that he  considered himself  uh intermediate to advanced like  intermediate two to advanced and he said  he speaks japanese well enough only well  enough to talk to his friends  well if you are intermediate  and you can only talk to your friends  then i think you have a severe  case of stage fright  uh i am quite happy to talk to anyone at  any stage of my  language development because my only  goal is to communicate and if i have  something to communicate i will  communicate even if i'm a ranked  beginner i don't spend a lot of time  talking as a beginner because i don't  have a lot to say i'm not able to say  very much but if the situation is there  if i have to communicate i communicate  with anyone not just with my friends  so there you have it on  pitch  stage fright and just staying relaxed  and natural and focusing on  communicating and being happy with  whatever level we have in the language  at every stage i'm happy  even at the beginning i'm happy i'm  doing a few things in the new language  and as i get better i'll continue to be  happy because i'm communicating  and and every so often i'll experience  certain breakthroughs i read a whole  book and i knew  most of the words  or whatever some sense of confirmation  that we are progressing  but as long as we focus on  communicating and enjoying the language  we will progress so don't be too uptight  out there don't worry about your pitch  bye for now
 

